WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:03.880
It’s the discovery of a lifetime:

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:08.900
Physicists have spotted gravitational waves, churned up by two black holes,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.740
locked in a death spiral deep in space.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.290
No one has seen such waves before

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:18.710
And they’re about to open new eyes on the cosmos.

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:24.460
Until now, scientists have learned about the
universe mainly by studying light and other

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:27.270
electromagnetic waves from space.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:31.740
But gravitational waves are ripples in space itself.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.270
They are set off by stellar cataclysms--

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:39.820
such as massive neutron stars 
or black holes whirling together.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:46.780
To sense the waves, physicists use massive
L-shaped optical devices called interferometers.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:51.140
Each arm of the L is 4 kilometers long.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.670
There are two of these devices in the United States.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:57.850
And together, they make up “LIGO”

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:03.649
To detect the stretching of space, LIGO uses
laser light to measure and compare the lengths

00:01:03.649 --> 00:01:05.720
of the two arms.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:10.660
When a gravitational wave ripples past, it  will stretch the arms by different amounts

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.120
causing the light to warble out of the interferometer.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:21.690
The measurements are sensitive -- 
to within 1/10,000 the width of a proton.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:27.040
Physicists hope to use the waves to test Albert Einstein's theory of gravity

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.180
And also to detect strange  objects that until now were completely invisible.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.880
Take the signal that LIGO detected.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.840
The black holes responsible are three times as massive as normal—

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.460
and nobody knows why.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.560
It’s just the first surprise from what promises to be

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.580
a whole new type of astronomy.

